#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678} :activity/id #uuid "6410482c-1a4b-55b8-afc6-f009af1b05ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175780 :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394119}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "c6666ad3-c461-5111-a9f4-d5a6f4f8e51a" :step/math-academy-id 20163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175781 :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493931}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "3ddd6359-72c0-5eee-87d9-df029bef4a21" :step/math-academy-id 20164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175782 :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8cbda370-20f2-5e6b-9780-b0122e4ee2a0" :step/math-academy-id 20166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176975 :tutorial/content "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix ![](images/a3/a351d711bda1d0b7009e0c2aa9ac8265e061e103f24dc2ff8c221711189a57e7.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = (1+\\textrm{i})x_1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $x_1\\in\\mathbb C$ to be the free variable, we find that the general solution to our system is given by\n\n![](images/8e/8eef5a23053809341f427e7099c364d65fa24d8144e4feac2a9d817accaa5a88.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/40/40c4333ba45ec2afa3479f682fcba72c2b61d69c4a4611b30b0d4078731e0a8b.svg)\n\nNotice that our eigenvector has complex-valued entries.\n\n**Note:** To check our result, we can show that $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v.$ Computing $A\\mathbf v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand computing $\\lambda \\mathbf v$ gives\n\n![](images/12/12fed2087f821e6e5da14d844b61e8300c8a4dc98ac4eff96f719fcc5795b5b4.svg)\n\nSo $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v,$ as required." :tutorial/id #uuid "1bde3a85-d8df-5194-8581-77fe2108627c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4297 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8f4ddd6c-a17e-54f3-bade-a64685e04b36" :step/math-academy-id 26123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176123}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411587} :activity/id #uuid "3099b967-b00c-5782-9a83-fd84f9ad579c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0489168e-93f4-5458-8a02-7bdcd1fa6253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f637c61-750b-5562-b8aa-12c7f8e8f1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349164} {:answer/id #uuid "733e337e-a48f-5c12-902f-9d1678192174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349506} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6761e2-57cb-5fcc-9965-269be85a60a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3ce77-31f6-5c5b-9f4d-8e85c892abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} :answer-field/id #uuid "674d76e0-0dad-52e1-9b92-041ef1633c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbfe9a5f-3d3d-54aa-9e82-2b9e587c84ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-81185" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "42de2582-1d6d-5484-ba6c-324ba0f7ff5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44822bc9-7e2e-562f-8058-69dd90f154f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375434} {:answer/id #uuid "a23d0c6f-90ba-502d-a418-481551cb71d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375435} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6d2e82-738d-5ce3-88ff-93e32f533bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375436} {:answer/id #uuid "696bc006-25c5-513a-bd9b-a6faa2dd9a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375437} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca94b17-5bb5-59b1-b428-73c7613972ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375438} :answer-field/id #uuid "16922444-299f-55a9-a2be-066c9885244c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ee811a8-c053-59bb-b00f-f224606c0564" :question/math-academy-id "q-81193" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(8) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 8 \\\\ -2 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(\\text{i})(-\\text{i})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "741564cd-c2f1-5aee-8b62-848c21e617d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f21ef-bd9e-5319-853e-7e3fc38e4a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411578} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc31227-522c-5e3c-865f-858998fd51d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411579} {:answer/id #uuid "b38f6e8b-7681-58f7-b559-5a8bae4dff01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411580} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc0bd2-9334-5cf0-9566-65f6c6862a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411581} {:answer/id #uuid "fa45e365-566e-56f7-88cc-77bf8156b5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c408a0-c9c5-54b6-81eb-ee9b40cb4448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db7154e6-6fda-5907-98f7-b3398371be54" :question/math-academy-id "q-38493" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7c22f243-0739-5a0f-8281-eeed84387bab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011ee86d-97e1-53d3-a3b6-63ede2abaf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "06c071d9-5963-5866-8063-d0604b2c6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269329} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b6a67-1956-5bd6-bf81-18c5c5717aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270464} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8004b4-6cab-5417-9d66-a6e51002060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5fd11-7f6b-58c9-94e4-6d1dc39b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} :answer-field/id #uuid "21591163-e34d-5795-bc94-436e957ba0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8845c35f-ffc0-56f1-b7af-e5bdef685e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4502c0c-e4ff-5fb4-9162-5b78360320c6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e967569-f453-574e-8919-a67fad95d2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324758} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6f6a10-0bd5-5a15-8100-84cdb1b48c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1224cc-38c3-56bc-8a03-7ccf158de176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325145} {:answer/id #uuid "ca079c89-eb27-58a1-8df2-a13f57b8062e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325722} {:answer/id #uuid "e251a6af-588a-5940-924b-907ba00680c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} :answer-field/id #uuid "d985c21d-6faa-5e92-8ab4-fa9bd7e30639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ea0f7da-291a-5ef5-afec-bc983b4386ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-39181" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "db758ac7-852d-51b4-ad30-e5acef4cfcd0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411585}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411577}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475991} :activity/id #uuid "9c7a70fd-abbe-516b-95be-41d0d36b0d99" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3952c809-b36e-5c0a-8130-4cc5c4fb5968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324433} {:answer/id #uuid "43c02d07-8760-5ae6-9e7a-56ace78318f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324516} {:answer/id #uuid "dda0e0ba-dab0-5877-a01a-860314071bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325905} {:answer/id #uuid "e7302783-d593-5c2c-812c-0a52e1e00077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326004} {:answer/id #uuid "ef81183b-9c91-5ee2-9a51-4f1ad44b92e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326083} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d219a79-bb65-5544-824f-9d942d15ccb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb7ad820-361f-5675-a375-d91857e5a50d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39188" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4638cfef-2e48-5d74-ab48-bf41cd68013c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242999fb-6645-5ccb-8016-d94893b454bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348760} {:answer/id #uuid "492ab36c-e111-5bf5-aecb-2c775badfc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349110} {:answer/id #uuid "497ebdce-d955-50f6-9c24-16eb97943ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} {:answer/id #uuid "8a583dc2-aca0-5635-8def-a18f404d931b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349701} {:answer/id #uuid "fafa63b9-44ba-538d-855c-38a2f923e2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349113} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b83764-dadd-53c8-9a1e-0eaa7017e5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7f8f4ed-a707-5ef8-a488-4f074f1c04ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-81031" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "532bd258-dd75-52e0-854c-9fc4d84a23df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007c15b8-5581-5d5e-b9b9-20eee41ea6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348458} {:answer/id #uuid "082db701-17a8-5bfd-8d51-0f9db11a73c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348516} {:answer/id #uuid "1b63928e-c54b-546f-a018-4d155b2013d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348679} {:answer/id #uuid "83473f2c-636b-50f3-bc7d-a4ffeaa05151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349648} {:answer/id #uuid "9be4dc92-527b-522b-8645-b3d9e5f62909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349834} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ba579e-2517-548c-a3d7-b08e597033da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8452eaa3-7ef9-5d96-b24f-03672a6ae137" :question/math-academy-id "q-81195" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "752a7cb9-f738-503d-976c-4799c9cb6901" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a00c60-8fe9-5976-9772-04b53a4f0cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269556} {:answer/id #uuid "28840bf8-728e-529f-b8ae-9e144824501a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269633} {:answer/id #uuid "4501ee0a-1e3d-5b90-8c4a-0918a3d52df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} {:answer/id #uuid "6b565f0d-2ee2-522d-abd4-444d8a903c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270223} {:answer/id #uuid "99f68ace-9f93-55a7-be71-674b44605a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b48799-b04a-51df-82be-a32acaaa2f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "015b39ab-328e-51c1-8903-e3d3a737ea7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140385" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "94fc7cb2-a156-54c9-af1e-6b820bdce7bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011ee86d-97e1-53d3-a3b6-63ede2abaf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "06c071d9-5963-5866-8063-d0604b2c6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269329} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b6a67-1956-5bd6-bf81-18c5c5717aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270464} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8004b4-6cab-5417-9d66-a6e51002060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5fd11-7f6b-58c9-94e4-6d1dc39b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} :answer-field/id #uuid "21591163-e34d-5795-bc94-436e957ba0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8845c35f-ffc0-56f1-b7af-e5bdef685e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f2a7d45a-7b9b-5db0-81a3-fa5d6f0775a5"}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475441}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494011} :activity/id #uuid "aa14eb4a-61b9-5764-91b0-789a38c1720c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c441ed-0435-5e2d-83a0-618a3112fcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348499} {:answer/id #uuid "45842989-10cc-5c73-ad09-63becc6e2b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349079} {:answer/id #uuid "4662c9e9-26e1-5ad9-933f-23f51efda70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349088} {:answer/id #uuid "5d944e2a-38b8-5891-a36e-9e4f87137e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349308} {:answer/id #uuid "806a25dd-fc2a-5451-8d21-5511e240733a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624} :answer-field/id #uuid "390a9ffd-1e61-5aac-8fcf-72786b0b334b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "931d7f2e-4fc8-5dd3-95f9-e9f514cf8458" :question/math-academy-id "q-81184" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad718acf-bc34-5af5-ab86-3a5dc856b0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366067} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c5ebb-e7a9-57dc-ac9a-df4eb523b425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} {:answer/id #uuid "7a336dcb-e67a-58d8-bb13-be3f3425f6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366069} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc1d8b9-1b3b-58d6-9550-65d587e7b93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366070} {:answer/id #uuid "53304843-707b-558e-bb98-11a42cfed664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} :answer-field/id #uuid "c472b1f5-b749-54e2-afc1-cb077c5b2380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "597496aa-43f1-55f6-97dc-9702fffad14f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38478" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493454} {:answer/id #uuid "4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493510} {:answer/id #uuid "7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493635} {:answer/id #uuid "af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81148" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{2}-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i}(4-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(4-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e6770c-7bf9-5f46-9062-0e30bf90a42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348592} {:answer/id #uuid "6a72311c-58cb-5b33-9b66-4446eb52c4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349429} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bf0977-5fa5-50f0-be87-c64eb8d03d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350045} {:answer/id #uuid "c7687cf8-d801-5c98-be83-eb0c3229d84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350202} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e115e-8718-54b9-9627-bd37a0f17f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e4857a-4c14-5c14-8928-81cdfeac6383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7207a8c6-6c0f-53ed-83a0-48cf577c32a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81027" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2373d10f-9177-5caa-aa21-81933675524a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324263} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0c4ffc-7cb8-5890-8b26-5fc5ba15fdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324333} {:answer/id #uuid "87d22551-71cd-562b-a2fb-e2c71dff8be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325128} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed941-102e-554e-9c2b-837d919cb7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} {:answer/id #uuid "f8014819-4600-55cf-996f-8ea4d1e77dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9f365-fbcc-5a5c-bd09-b46393c41e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d55b44c8-8d42-5261-b22a-10b38f009304" :question/math-academy-id "q-38494" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494019}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493385}]}
